WEBVTT
NOTE why-does-steak-taste-better-seared - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.325
A wet steak cannot brown until its surface
climbs past boiling water.

00:00:04.325 --> 00:00:10.018
Searing tastes better because a dry, very
hot surface drives the Maillard reaction,

00:00:10.018 --> 00:00:15.436
in which amino acids and sugars in the
meat recombine into brown pigments and a

00:00:15.436 --> 00:00:20.992
large family of new aroma compounds that
raw or gently cooked meat does not have.

00:00:20.992 --> 00:00:25.735
The reaction carries the name of
Louis-Camille Maillard, who described it

00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:28.464
to the French Academy of Sciences in 1912.

00:00:28.464 --> 00:00:32.863
That date does not explain why a frying
pan produces this flavour on meat and a

00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:34.645
pot of simmering water does not.

00:00:34.645 --> 00:00:39.043
The answer is mostly about water, and
about the places on a steak where water

00:00:39.043 --> 00:00:39.728
cannot stay.

00:00:39.728 --> 00:00:41.845
Why does steak taste better seared?

00:00:41.845 --> 00:00:44.044
Look at a seared steak cut in half.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:48.944
On screen is a dry crust over a wet
interior, and the two layers cooked in the

00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:49.509
same pan.

00:00:49.509 --> 00:00:53.068
The thin outer layer is brown, firm and
fragrant.

00:00:53.068 --> 00:00:56.917
A few millimetres in, the meat is pink and
still wet.

00:00:56.917 --> 00:01:00.089
Those two zones did not simply cook to
different degrees.

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:02.203
They went through different chemistry.

00:01:02.203 --> 00:01:06.290
The interior was heated, and its proteins
changed shape and tightened.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:11.019
The crust was transformed: molecules that
were there in the raw meat reacted with

00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:14.464
each other and became molecules that were
not there before.

00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:16.856
That distinction is the whole episode.

00:01:16.856 --> 00:01:21.829
It is tempting to think a crust tastes
stronger because it is concentrated, the

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:24.347
way a sauce gets stronger as it reduces.

00:01:24.347 --> 00:01:29.315
Some of that happens; drying does
concentrate what is already present.

00:01:29.315 --> 00:01:32.437
But concentration cannot explain new
smells.

00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:36.353
Raw beef has a faint, metallic, slightly
sweet aroma.

00:01:36.353 --> 00:01:39.973
A seared crust smells roasted, nutty and
savoury.

00:01:39.973 --> 00:01:44.761
Those notes come from compounds that have
to be built, and building them needs a

00:01:44.761 --> 00:01:47.395
specific reaction under specific
conditions.

00:01:47.395 --> 00:01:49.429
Maillard was not studying steak.

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:54.767
He was a physician and chemist interested
in how amino acids, the building blocks of

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:55.848
proteins, behave.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:02:01.140
In his report to the French Academy of
Sciences in 1912, he described heating

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.569
solutions of amino acids with sugars and
watching them turn yellow, then brown,

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:08.493
while giving off new odours.

00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:13.653
The important part of his observation was
that neither ingredient did this on its

00:02:13.653 --> 00:02:16.456
own at those temperatures. It took the
pair.

00:02:16.456 --> 00:02:21.138
Chemists now define the Maillard reaction
as a network of reactions between amino

00:02:21.138 --> 00:02:25.474
groups, usually on amino acids or
proteins, and reducing sugars, a class of

00:02:25.474 --> 00:02:28.653
sugars with a reactive group that can open
up and bond.

00:02:28.653 --> 00:02:33.012
The American Chemical Society, which
publishes extensively on this chemistry,

00:02:33.012 --> 00:02:35.389
treats it as distinct from caramelization.

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:39.235
Caramelization is what sugar does alone
when it is heated hard.

00:02:39.235 --> 00:02:44.180
Meat contains very little free sugar, so a
steak crust is overwhelmingly Maillard

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.843
chemistry, with caramelization playing at
most a minor part.

00:02:47.843 --> 00:02:51.043
That raises an obvious question about the
meat.

00:02:51.043 --> 00:02:55.741
If there is so little sugar, where does
the reaction get its partner?

00:02:55.741 --> 00:03:01.031
Muscle contains small amounts of glucose
and, importantly, ribose, a sugar that is

00:03:01.031 --> 00:03:04.579
part of the energy-carrying molecules
muscle cells use.

00:03:04.579 --> 00:03:09.715
Small quantities are enough, because each
reaction can feed many downstream ones.

00:03:09.715 --> 00:03:11.823
Here is where the pan matters.

00:03:11.823 --> 00:03:17.235
Chemistry explainers from the American
Chemical Society describe the Maillard

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:22.787
reaction as slow at ordinary cooking
temperatures and speeding up sharply above

00:03:22.787 --> 00:03:27.917
roughly 140 degrees Celsius, while a wet
surface stalls near 100 degrees.

00:03:27.917 --> 00:03:31.974
Treat that 140 as a convenient landmark
rather than a switch.

00:03:31.974 --> 00:03:36.763
The reaction does occur at lower
temperatures, very slowly, which is why

00:03:36.763 --> 00:03:39.091
some foods brown over long storage.

00:03:39.091 --> 00:03:43.818
In a kitchen timeframe of minutes, though,
browning needs a surface well above the

00:03:43.818 --> 00:03:45.144
boiling point of water.

00:03:45.144 --> 00:03:47.422
And a wet surface cannot get there.

00:03:47.422 --> 00:03:52.562
While liquid water sits on the meat, the
energy arriving from the pan goes into

00:03:52.562 --> 00:03:54.515
turning that water into steam.

00:03:54.515 --> 00:03:58.136
The surface temperature is pinned close to
boiling until the water is gone.

00:03:58.136 --> 00:04:02.948
This is why a steak dropped into simmering
stock turns grey, not brown, however long

00:04:02.948 --> 00:04:03.464
it cooks.

00:04:03.464 --> 00:04:08.434
It is also why a crowded pan steams
instead of searing: the meat releases

00:04:08.434 --> 00:04:11.565
moisture faster than the pan can drive it
off.

00:04:11.565 --> 00:04:15.148
So the sequence on screen is a chain of
conditions.

00:04:15.148 --> 00:04:17.747
The hot pan boils away surface water.

00:04:17.747 --> 00:04:18.901
The surface dries.

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:24.097
Only then can its temperature climb past
boiling, and only then does the Maillard

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:28.844
reaction speed up enough to build a crust
in the minutes before the inside

00:04:28.844 --> 00:04:29.485
overcooks.

00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:34.077
A sear is a race between drying the
outside and heating the inside.

00:04:34.077 --> 00:04:38.730
A very hot pan wins that race because it
dries the surface quickly while the

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.016
interior lags behind.

00:04:40.016 --> 00:04:44.405
Octopuses impress me. So much of their
nervous system runs through their arms.

00:04:44.405 --> 00:04:49.001
They aren't just unusual — they look like
a completely different solution to

00:04:49.001 --> 00:04:49.787
intelligence.

00:04:49.787 --> 00:04:54.508
For four decades after Maillard, browning
was a well-known effect without a clear

00:04:54.508 --> 00:04:54.741
map.

00:04:54.741 --> 00:05:00.142
In 1953, John Hodge, a chemist at a USDA
research laboratory in Peoria, Illinois,

00:05:00.142 --> 00:05:05.010
published a scheme in the Journal of
Agricultural and Food Chemistry that

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.611
organised the reaction into a sequence.

00:05:07.611 --> 00:05:11.610
His paper became one of the most cited in
food chemistry, because it gave

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.829
researchers a framework for asking which
step produced which colour or smell.

00:05:15.829 --> 00:05:21.396
Hodge divided the reaction into an early
stage, an intermediate stage and a final

00:05:21.396 --> 00:05:21.808
stage.

00:05:21.808 --> 00:05:27.054
In the early stage, a sugar and an amino
group join and rearrange into a more

00:05:27.054 --> 00:05:28.416
stable intermediate.

00:05:28.416 --> 00:05:31.391
Nothing is brown yet, and little has an
aroma.

00:05:31.391 --> 00:05:36.047
In the intermediate stage, those
intermediates break apart and recombine

00:05:36.047 --> 00:05:38.181
into smaller, reactive fragments.

00:05:38.181 --> 00:05:40.295
Many of the smells appear here.

00:05:40.295 --> 00:05:45.887
In the final stage, fragments link into
large brown polymers, the pigments you see

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:46.773
as the crust.

00:05:46.773 --> 00:05:49.436
The scheme mattered because it was
testable.

00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:54.035
If a smell comes from the intermediate
stage, you should be able to block or

00:05:54.035 --> 00:05:58.633
boost it by changing the conditions that
govern that stage, such as acidity,

00:05:58.633 --> 00:06:01.477
moisture or the particular amino acid
involved.

00:06:01.477 --> 00:06:06.862
Decades of model experiments, mixing one
sugar with one amino acid and heating

00:06:06.862 --> 00:06:08.864
them, have done exactly that.

00:06:08.864 --> 00:06:13.401
The broad outline Hodge drew has held,
although later work showed the network is

00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:16.464
far more branched than a three-stage
diagram suggests.

00:06:16.464 --> 00:06:20.707
Knowing that browning makes smells is
different from knowing which smells.

00:06:20.707 --> 00:06:22.656
That answer came from instruments.

00:06:22.656 --> 00:06:27.366
Gas chromatography separates the volatile
molecules escaping from cooked meat,

00:06:27.366 --> 00:06:32.016
sending them through a long, thin column
where they emerge one after another.

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:36.619
Mass spectrometry then identifies each one
by how it breaks into fragments.

00:06:36.619 --> 00:06:41.531
And a sniffing port, where a trained
person smells the gas leaving the column,

00:06:41.531 --> 00:06:43.483
links each molecule to a smell.

00:06:43.483 --> 00:06:48.500
That last step matters, because the most
abundant compounds are often not the ones

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.173
you notice.

00:06:49.173 --> 00:06:53.396
Studies of this kind, many published in
the Journal of Agricultural and Food

00:06:53.396 --> 00:06:57.285
Chemistry, have catalogued a large number
of volatiles in cooked beef.

00:06:57.285 --> 00:07:02.272
Published counts vary with the method and
the cut, so no single total is worth

00:07:02.272 --> 00:07:02.784
quoting.

00:07:02.784 --> 00:07:07.350
Several families keep recurring:
pyrazines, with roasted and nutty notes;

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.728
furans; and sulfur-containing compounds
with savoury, meaty character.

00:07:11.728 --> 00:07:16.834
PubChem, the public chemical database,
lists one of them, 2-methyl-3-furanthiol,

00:07:16.834 --> 00:07:21.621
which forms when the sulfur-bearing amino
acid cysteine reacts with ribose.

00:07:21.621 --> 00:07:26.598
Small amounts of compounds like it carry
an outsized share of what people call

00:07:26.598 --> 00:07:26.981
meaty.

00:07:26.981 --> 00:07:29.197
One more process runs alongside.

00:07:29.197 --> 00:07:34.805
Strecker degradation, a side reaction of
the Maillard network, breaks amino acids

00:07:34.805 --> 00:07:38.059
into small aldehydes with malty, toasty
smells.

00:07:38.059 --> 00:07:42.384
It is another reason the crust smells of
more than the meat it came from.

00:07:42.384 --> 00:07:46.789
Many of these molecules are perceived by
smell rather than by the tongue, often

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:49.131
through the back of the mouth as you chew.

00:07:49.131 --> 00:07:54.299
When people say a seared steak tastes
better, much of what they detect is aroma.

00:07:54.299 --> 00:07:58.841
There is a popular explanation that
searing locks in juices, and it does not

00:07:58.841 --> 00:07:59.797
survive testing.

00:07:59.797 --> 00:08:04.606
Cooks and food scientists have weighed
steaks before and after cooking, comparing

00:08:04.606 --> 00:08:07.099
ones seared first with ones that were not.

00:08:07.099 --> 00:08:12.098
Seared steaks lose about as much moisture,
and sometimes more, because the surface

00:08:12.098 --> 00:08:13.867
reaches a higher temperature.

00:08:13.867 --> 00:08:15.097
The crust is porous.

00:08:15.097 --> 00:08:19.897
The sizzle you hear the whole time is
water escaping as steam, which is direct

00:08:19.897 --> 00:08:22.235
evidence that nothing has been sealed.

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:26.827
This is worth holding onto, because it
shows how the right explanation was

00:08:26.827 --> 00:08:31.461
The sealing idea predicted something
measurable, a heavier steak, and the scale

00:08:31.461 --> 00:08:32.048
disagreed.

00:08:32.048 --> 00:08:37.035
The Maillard explanation predicts other
measurable things: no browning while the

00:08:37.035 --> 00:08:42.147
surface is wet, faster browning at higher
temperatures, and specific new compounds

00:08:42.147 --> 00:08:42.957
in the crust.

00:08:42.957 --> 00:08:44.950
Each of those has been observed.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:49.619
The question is answered by what weighs,
smells and separates as predicted.

00:08:49.619 --> 00:08:52.409
The Maillard reaction is not alone in the
pan.

00:08:52.409 --> 00:08:55.320
Fat on and in the meat also changes when
heated.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.062
Its components oxidise and break into
fragments with their own aromas, and some

00:09:00.062 --> 00:09:05.105
of those fragments then join the Maillard
network, reacting with amino acids to make

00:09:05.105 --> 00:09:07.987
compounds neither process would make
separately.

00:09:07.987 --> 00:09:12.454
Researchers who compare beef with other
meats attribute much of the difference

00:09:12.454 --> 00:09:17.035
between them to fat chemistry, while the
roasted character common to all of them

00:09:17.035 --> 00:09:18.925
traces back to Maillard browning.

00:09:18.925 --> 00:09:22.515
When the edit cuts to the pan, notice the
order of events.

00:09:22.515 --> 00:09:26.223
First there is steam and noise, as surface
water boils off.

00:09:26.223 --> 00:09:28.675
Only after that does the colour deepen.

00:09:28.675 --> 00:09:33.149
The visual delay between sizzle and brown
is the experiment playing out in real

00:09:33.149 --> 00:09:36.435
time, and the sequence of the cuts should
keep that order.

00:09:36.435 --> 00:09:41.949
This evidence explains which molecules a
sear creates and why a dry, hot surface is

00:09:41.949 --> 00:09:43.277
needed to make them.

00:09:43.277 --> 00:09:47.901
It does not establish that seared steak is
better in any absolute sense.

00:09:47.901 --> 00:09:52.033
Preference is learned and varies between
people and cuisines.

00:09:52.033 --> 00:09:56.909
The chemistry also comes from model
systems and measured samples; a real

00:09:56.909 --> 00:10:02.192
steak, with its own cut, fat and history,
will not match any published profile

00:10:02.192 --> 00:10:02.733
exactly.

00:10:02.733 --> 00:10:04.366
A wet steak cannot brown.

00:10:04.366 --> 00:10:09.461
Dry it, heat it past boiling, and a
reaction first seen in a test tube in 1912

00:10:09.461 --> 00:10:13.053
begins building flavour that was never in
the raw meat.
